    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense, but I'm still struggling with the \"suddenness\" of it. Even when I keep the temperature fairly constant, it feels like the starter just goes rogue overnight. Is there a specific threshold or a certain ratio of yeast to bacteria where the culture becomes inherently unstable and shifts toward that high acidity so abruptly? Like, is there a tipping point where the microbes just stop behaving predictably?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "There isn't a single, precise threshold like a specific ratio, as stability is very dynamic, but rapid swings often occur when the dominant acid-producers, like certain *Lactobacillus* strains, overcome the initial buffering capacity of the starter. This \"rogue\" behavior suggests an imbalance where the bacterial growth rate has suddenly spiked to consume flour resources far faster than the slower-acting yeast can keep pace. If you are feeding a starter that is too hungry or has reached a peak of acid production, it becomes hypersensitive to minor environmental nudges. Consistency in feeding ratios (e.g., always using a 1:2:2 ratio) can help prevent these sudden shifts.",
