      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, you can visualize this as a shift from cooperative to competitive dominance within the microbial community. Initially, yeast and bacteria exhibit a balanced relationship, where yeast creates initial conditions and bacteria thrive. However, if the feeding rate or maturity drifts, one group—usually the acid-producing *Lactobacillus*—can begin to outcompete the others for available nutrients. This aggressive dominance triggers the chaotic phase because the byproduct accumulation (excess acid) inhibits the very organisms needed to maintain equilibrium. Monitoring the vigor of the rise and the acidity over time is the best proxy for detecting this internal competitive imbalance.",
